## If this process dies, what happens to the run?

*“Let it crash”* works because a supervisor can start a new process in a known good state. But for a GenServer, that known good state is whatever init/1 builds. For a workflow, that means starting from the beginning, which is exactly what we don’t want.

Think about our endpoint. By the time the process crashes, the model may have already made several decisions: it read the router, read the controller, ran the tests, maybe even edited the controller. Starting over means repeating all of that, paying for it again in time and in model calls. And some of that work, like editing a file, may not be something we want to do twice.

The process can own the workflow state while it’s running, but that doesn’t mean process memory has to be the only place where that state lives.

We need enough durable information to reconstruct the workflow.

One way to do that is to record its progress as an append-only log. The goal is the first entry. Each tool call the model decides on becomes another, as does each tool result. As the workflow progresses, we only ever add these entries to the end.

For our endpoint, the log might look like this when the process crashes:

```
{:goal, "The GET /users/:id endpoint is returning a 500 error..."}
{:decision, 1, {:read_file, %{path: "lib/my_app_web/router.ex"}}}
{:result, 1, ~s(get "/users/:id", UserController, :show)}
{:decision, 2, {:read_file, %{path: "lib/my_app_web/controllers/user_controller.ex"}}}
{:decision, 3, {:run_tests, %{path: "test/my_app_web/controllers/user_controller_test.exs"}}}
{:result, 2, ~s[def show(conn, %{"id" => id}) do ... json(conn, user) ...]}
```

Everything the process knew is there: the goal, every decision, every result that came back. Even the request that’s still waiting for its answer.

If that log becomes the source from which we reconstruct the workflow state, we’re using the same core idea as **event sourcing**.
